    "result_text": "Both changes verified. Here's my assessment of the two findings:\n\n## Finding 1 \u2014 `govern: [ transaction ]` on the logging step: **Agreed, fixed**\n\n`AuditOwnerCreated` touches no repository \u2014 it only reads the already-in-memory `@Val Owner` and emits a log line. Per AGENTS.md, `govern: [ transaction ]` marks steps that participate in a DB write, so tagging a pure-logging step is misleading and needlessly extends the write transaction across the logging call. I dropped `govern:` from the `audit` step. The transaction now closes after `save`, and the audit observes the committed create. Tests stay green and the audit line still emits correctly (`AUDIT - owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-29`).\n\nNote that `respond` legitimately keeps its `govern:` \u2014 its DTO mapping can touch lazy-loaded associations and needs the persistence context open \u2014 so this isn't just blanket \"govern everything\"; the audit step genuinely doesn't qualify.\n\n## Finding 2 \u2014 rename `service` \u2192 `audit`: **Wrong, left as-is**\n\nThe finding's premise doesn't hold for this codebase. It claims siblings use verb-named methods (`validate`, `load`, `save`, `respond`), but that's the pattern in the AGENTS.md *worked example*, not the actual converted code. Every one of the 26 public methods in the owner function package is named `service`:\n\n```\n     26 service\n```\n\n`SaveOwner.service`, `RespondWithOwnerCreated.service`, `AssignOwnerCustomerCode.service`, etc. Renaming mine to `audit` would make it the *only* class breaking the established local idiom \u2014 the opposite of the finding's stated goal of frictionless reading. The class name `AuditOwnerCreated` already conveys the verb; the method stays `service` to match its 26 siblings.\n\nFull suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**, 1 test, 0 failures.",
